{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/987696-small-scale-weather-manipulation","id":987696,"title":"Small Scale Weather Manipulation","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-10-15T03:42:45-07:00","id":987696,"title":"Small Scale Weather Manipulation","content":"We all know that Forces 4+ is used to manipulate the weather on a large scale. Because I suppose the movement of air, water, ice, dust/sand, and the like is the province of Forces, even if their material components are the realm of Matter. The latter isn't needed for an Effect, so long as the Mage is moving around material that is already there.<br /> <br /> But none of the books I've read cover whether a Mage can affect the weather on the small scale. Wind is obviously covered, but what of mist and fog? Can a Mage create a sheet of fog the size of a room with Forces 3 + Prime 2? If there isn't enough moisture in the air, would the Mage need to include Matter 2 to that same Effect? What about summoning a localized rain cloud inside a room?<br /> <br /> Edit: Actually, I thought of something else. The Prime 2 element above is used to create the movement force on the water molecules from nothing. But if one used Forces 3 alone, they could convert heat into that force, suspending the water in the air while making it as a foggy autumn morning. Or, if the Mage's Paradigm allowed for it, to convert the Cold force into that motive energy, simultaneously moving the water and turning the room into a sauna. The heat would even prevent the water from condensing for a while, so it lasts long after the magick itself ended.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":6156,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","user":"Bluecho","date":". 10-15-2016, 06:49 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-17T21:40:57-07:00","id":989194,"title":"","content":"Responding to your paragraphs in reverse order:<br /> <br /> I don't know what you're talking about in the &quot;Edit&quot; paragraph.<br /> <br /> Yes, you would need Matter to summon water vapor if there is no water nearby to vaporize.<br /> <br /> I would say that you can use Forces 2 or 3 to create small weather effects. I see the Forces 2/3 vs 4/5 divide as mostly a matter of degree, not kind. Like you can't split atoms until at least Adept level, but that's a huge amount of energy input. I disagree with the books that you can't affect gravity's affect on individual targets until Forces 4. It is the weakest of the fundamental forces of the universe.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-17T23:19:03-07:00","id":989231,"title":"","content":"What I mean is that, for Forces to generate mist or fog (assuming there is water vapor already present in the environment), it needs to apply kinetic energy to the water. This being to move the vapor from wherever it is, into the open air. Since this kinetic energy is a force, a Mage using Forces 3 ought to be able to use it alone to change some ambient force - like heat or cold - into the kinetic force needed to accomplish the Effect. Ergo, so long as there is water already present on location, it is possible to create mist or fog (or even localized rain clouds) without the aid of Prime or Matter.<br /> <br /> I further postulated that the very act of siphoning heat (or cold) from the air to fuel said Effect could, by its very nature, produce conditions more favorable for the weather effect to persist after the magick dissipated. Diverting cold from the location would make it warmer, and thus harder for water to condense into its liquid state.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-18T08:10:47-07:00","id":989353,"title":"","content":"Assuming your mage actually understands what they are doing (using the \u201cDude, do you even know what you\u2019re doing\u201d concept from HDYDT p28), Forces 3 should be enough to make fog. If there isn\u2019t enough water in the air present, then you may need Matter 2 to make more water. There should almost always be enough (something) present that you can use Matter 2 without Prime 2 to create water out of nothing.<br /> <br /> Here is where you would really want Matter. Forces can create fog, and you can use Forces to push fog around just like you had a fan, but if you want an effect where you control the fog, then you should use Matter for locking. With Forces 3 Matter 2 you can make the fog form and shape it however you desire.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2900-frostbyte000jm","name":"frostbyte000jm","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"62"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-18T15:53:35-07:00","id":989584,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>frostbyte000jm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/987696-small-scale-weather-manipulation?p=989353#post989353\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Assuming your mage actually understands what they are doing (using the \u201cDude, do you even know what you\u2019re doing\u201d concept from HDYDT p28), Forces 3 should be enough to make fog. If there isn\u2019t enough water in the air present, then you may need Matter 2 to make more water. There should almost always be enough (something) present that you can use Matter 2 without Prime 2 to create water out of nothing.<br /> <br /> Here is where you would really want Matter. Forces can create fog, and you can use Forces to push fog around just like you had a fan, but if you want an effect where you control the fog, then you should use Matter for locking. With Forces 3 Matter 2 you can make the fog form and shape it however you desire.</div> </div> </div> </div>First, you cannot create something from nothing without Prime 2. That's been a part of the rules since forever.<br /> <br /> Second, true enough about Matter, but that assumes we're running with that rule. And ever since M20 came out, I've had a personal disagreement with the entire concept of &quot;locking&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-18T16:58:04-07:00","id":989618,"title":"","content":"Maybe I said It poorly. I agree you need Prime 2 to create something from nothing. <br /> <br /> I am saying in this case, you should never have nothing. There should always be moisture in the air. Unless something weird is happening. <br /> <br /> If you know what you're doing, using forces should work. You could know how to properly heat up that moisture and make it become fog. You're getting to that effect through science. Prime shouldn't be necessary, in this case. <br /> <br /> I never knew locking wasn't a thing, tho we never called it locking till M20 seem to give it a name. But this is a different tangent, so I will throw this part out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2900-frostbyte000jm","name":"frostbyte000jm","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"62"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}